# 📊 Benchmark configuration for Pingclair — the DSL twin of
# `benchmarks/aws-h3/configs/config-pingclair-aws-proxy.json`.
#
# 🧾 Why this file exists: the JSON it replaces skips `adapter/caddyfile.rs`
# entirely, so every benchmark run measured about half the path a real
# operator's configuration takes — and the missing half is the one that
# decides what the compiled handlers actually are. A number measured on a
# shape nobody can write in the DSL is a number about something else.
#
# ⚖️ Fairness notes, so the comparison against nginx and pingap stays honest:
#
# - `compress` is OFF. The nginx and pingap benchmark configs do not compress
#   either, and a 1 KiB body would spend more time being deflated than sent.
#   (The general-purpose `configs/pingclair/Pingclairfile` DOES enable gzip;
#   it is not the benchmark config, and using it here would have measured
#   Pingclair doing work the others were not asked to do.)
# - `flush_interval -1` matches the JSON: flush every write straight through
#   rather than buffering, which is what nginx's `proxy_buffering off` does.
# - Two knobs the JSON sets explicitly have no DSL spelling yet:
#   `worker_threads` and `upstream_keepalive_pool_size`. Both are omitted here
#   rather than worked around, because their defaults already are the values
#   the JSON names: threads follow `available_parallelism()` (2 in a 2-vCPU
#   container) and the pool defaults to 512 (`run.rs`). Verified, not assumed
#   — see the equivalence check in the run log.
#
# 🔥 A third knob is a real difference, not a matching default. The JSON sets
#   `"metrics": false`; the DSL's `metrics` is an on-only toggle and the
#   compiled default is ON. So this file cannot turn metrics off, and every
#   Pingclair configured from a Pingclairfile — which is every real operator —
#   pays whatever per-request cost metrics collection has, while the numbers
#   published on 2026-08-03 were measured with it off. That makes those
#   numbers a measurement of a configuration nobody can write. Recorded in
#   TRIAGE.md on 2026-08-11; this run measures the shape operators actually
#   get, and the difference from the old figures is therefore expected and
#   is itself a finding.
#
# 🌐 The upstream is the container network alias `bench-backend` rather than
#   the JSON's `127.0.0.1:9000`. The AWS harness ran the backend on the same
#   host; every container harness (OrbStack here, and the x86 image sent to
#   the old AMD box) needs a name. nginx and pingap are pointed at the same
#   alias, so no candidate gets a shorter path to the backend than another.
#
# 🚫 One deliberate difference from the JSON: it sets `index: []` and this
#   sets the DSL default (`index.html`). The workload only ever requests
#   `/small.txt` and `/proxy/small.txt`, so no request reaches index
#   resolution. Called out rather than hidden, because it is a difference.

{
	auto_https off
}

# 🔓 Plaintext listener, used by the HTTP/1.1 `wrk` rows.
http://bench.local:8080 {
	handle /proxy/* {
		reverse_proxy http://bench-backend:9000 {
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}

	file_server /var/www/html
}

# 🔐 TLS listener, used by every `h2load` row (H2 and H1S) and by HTTP/3.
https://h3.local:8443 {
	tls /tmp/bench.crt /tmp/bench.key

	handle /proxy/* {
		reverse_proxy http://bench-backend:9000 {
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}

	file_server /var/www/html
}
